#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Notes on the literature]
<notes:local-residues>

#notes-on(<sec:local-residue>) The main results of this section with different
proofs are in the book of Griffiths and Harris @bib:Griffiths1978a[Chapter~5].
The transformation formula for a local residue occurs in the earlier works of
Hartshorne @bib:Hartshorne1966 and Tong @bib:Tong1973; some special cases were
obtained by Weil @bib:Weil1935 (with integration over the distinguished boundary
of a polyhedron) and Aizenberg @bib:Aizenberg1964 (with integration over the
boundary of a domain). The formula @eq:kytmanov of
@ss:transformation-formula[subsection] was proved by Kytmanov. The idea of the
proof of the proposition in @ss:boundary-representation[subsection] was
suggested to the author by Yuzhakov. Proofs of the results of
@ss:geometric-multiplicity[subsection] are in the book of Aizenberg and Yuzhakov
@bib:Aizenberg1979b[§2]. The local duality theorem is due to Grothendieck
@bib:Grothendieck1957 (see also @bib:Hartshorne1966). Examples of analytic
functionals that cannot be realized by local residues were mentioned by Bakhov.

#notes-on(<sec:trace>) The logarithmic residue formula of
@ss:geometric-trace[subsection] for $n = 2$ was cited by Caccioppoli
@bib:Caccioppoli1949 ($phi equiv 1$) and Martinelli @bib:Martinelli1955, and for
$n > 2$ by Bishop @bib:Bishop1961 and Sorani @bib:Sorani1960. The assertions of
@ss:residue-trace[subsection] are also in @bib:Arnold1982[vol.~1, §5]. The
theorem of @ss:bishop[subsection] is due to Bishop @bib:Bishop1961, and
Propositions~@prop:finite-extension and~@prop:algebraic-trace were obtained by
the author under the influence of the algebraic works @bib:VanDerWaerden1971,
@bib:Lomadze1981, @bib:Parshin1976, @bib:Beauville1971. We should observe that
in the more abstract situation when $k$ is a field and $K$ is a commutative
$k$-algebra with unity, a residue in the space of differentials $Omega_(K\/k)^1$
was defined by Tate @bib:Tate1968.

#notes-on(<sec:manifold>) The theorem on the total sum of local residues and its
converse were first proved by Griffiths @bib:Griffiths1976. Independent proofs
of this theorem in certain special cases were given in the papers of Khovanskii
@bib:Khovanskii1978 and Tsikh @bib:Tsikh1979. The topological proof we have
given follows @bib:Tsikh1979.

#notes-on(<sec:total-sum>) The lemma on a "displaced" distinguished boundary and
the Rouché principle for residues were proved by Yuzhakov @bib:Yuzhakov1975a.
Formula @eq:laurent-formula of @ss:laurent-expansion[subsection] was obtained by
Aizenberg and Tsikh @bib:Aizenberg1979a. An algorithm for calculating the global
residue was given in a paper by Yuzhakov @bib:Yuzhakov1984b.

#notes-on(<sec:representability>) The main result of this section (the theorem
of @ss:separating-cycles[subsection]) was proved by Tsikh @bib:Tsikh1975,
@bib:Tsikh1984b. Special cases of this theorem have been obtained by a number of
authors. Didon @bib:Didon1873 showed that the integral $integral_Gamma omega$ of
the logarithmic differential $omega = (d f_1 \/ f_1) and (d f_2 \/ f_2)$ over
any two-dimensional closed contour $Gamma$ in $CC^2$ that does not intersect
singularities of $omega$ is equal to an integer. Picard @bib:Picard1926 noted
that even for polynomials $f_1$ and $f_2$ the integral $integral_Gamma omega$
does not always express the number of roots of the system of equations
$f_1 = f_2 = 0$. Moreover, Fantappiè @bib:Fantappie1931 assumed that the
integral is connected with the number of roots if the cycle $Gamma$ is
separating. Martinelli @bib:Martinelli1955 confirmed this conjecture for the
case when $Gamma$ is situated on a small sphere encircling one of the roots.
Sorani @bib:Sorani1960, @bib:Sorani1963 showed that for the #source(74) specific
cycles $Gamma = {|f_1| = |f_2| = epsilon}$ the integral $integral_Gamma omega$
expresses the number of zeros in the corresponding domain, so he assumed (for
any number of variables) that separating cycles must be expressed in terms of
such specific cycles. This conjecture was confirmed by Yuzhakov
@bib:Yuzhakov1970 in the case when the hypersurfaces $F_j = {f_j = 0}$ are
submanifolds of general position in a Stein manifold. The results of
@ss:separation-coefficients[subsection] were obtained by Tsikh in
@bib:Tsikh1985c, @bib:Tsikh1986, and the theorem of
@ss:many-hypersurfaces[subsection] is due to Yuzhakov.

#notes-on(<sec:parameter>) The contents of this section were described in a
lecture course by the author @bib:Tsikh1986.
